## Coordinator — apply the verifier's standard to yourself before signalling VERIFY

Convergence is real: 10 findings → 4 → 2 → 1 → 1, and the last two candidates were minimal, in-scope diffs with no over-scope work. But the remaining findings all share one shape, and you can close that whole class in a single cycle instead of one per round.

The standard the verifier applies is **mutation testing**: a mechanism has executable proof only if **removing or inverting it makes at least one test fail**. Both of the last two blocking findings were found exactly that way — the FIFO fixture passed regardless of insertion order, and the suite stayed green with `self._automatic_release and` deleted from line 197.

**Before your next `VERIFY`, run that standard against yourself across the whole contract.** For each binding rule and acceptance criterion, delete or invert the mechanism that implements it and confirm the suite goes red. Concretely, at minimum:

- binding rule 1 no-polling → the ready-view read counter assertion;
- binding rule 2 automatic release ON/OFF, both the profile switch and the imported-view flag, independently;
- binding rule 3 priority is never reordered;
- binding rule 4 each of the three enumerated stop reasons;
- binding rules 6–8 custody: the digest check, the independent read-back, the copy the verifier retains;
- AC 2 priority ordering, FIFO among equal, FIFO among absent — each separately;
- AC 3 dependency predicate; AC 4 WIP admission refusal; AC 5 restart reconciliation; AC 7 rework and authority-block routes; AC 10 evidence-less ACCEPT refusal; AC 11 no repeated reads.

Any mechanism whose removal leaves the suite green has no discriminating proof yet. Fix those in one pass.

**Constraints unchanged:** extend, never replace (SWF-23 §2 — `test_offline_profile_can_disable_automatic_release` stays, it just needs a sibling whose fixture makes the profile flag decisive); no production change beyond what a finding requires; no scope expansion (SWF-20); PY-02 untouched.

Report the mutation results with your next candidate so the verifier can check the claim rather than rediscover it.
